# Evolutionary Rate Analysis through dN/dS ratio

This project analyzes the selecctive pressure acting over the Olig2 gene through the ratio of non-synonymous to synonymous substitutions (dN/dS), and comparing the Olig2 value against the dN/dS values of the human genome, the human transcription factors values and the human bHLH family values. 
The dN/dS ratio has been chosen for this comparison because it informs about the strength and mode of natural selection that act over a coding gene. The dN/dS value is calculated as the quotient of the dN value, which refers to the rate of substitutions at non-synonymous sites in a sequence; and the dS value; which refers to the rate of substitutions at synonymous sites.

The raw data used has been added to this repository, and it consists of the genes’ dN and dS values and its Ensemble ID, retrieved from the version “Ensembl 77: Oct 2014”, of the “Ensembl Archives”. The database chosen was “Ensembl Gene 77”, the chosen dataset was “ Homo sapiens genes (GRCh38)” and the species selected as orthologs were “Chimpanzee” and “Mouse”. The species chimpanzee was selected due to its closeness to humans and the species mouse was chosen  assess the selective pressure across a longer evolutionary distance.
To conduct the comparison between the Olig2 dN/dS value and the human transcription factors values, it is necessary to add the file "HumanTFs_DBD.txt", which contains information about the Ensembl ID of the human transcription factors. 

### Example Output
The script generates a violin plot that shows the distribution of the dN/dS ratio when comparing human and chimpanzee and human and mouse orthologs for three groups of genes: the whole genome, human transcription factors and the bHLH family genes. In the three violin plots, the position of the Olig2 dN/dS value was marked in the distribution with a red horizontal line.
In addition, the script computes the mean and meadian of each gene group for both species and the percentile in which the dN/dS ratio of the Olig2 gene falls

